## Origin & Evolution
The J.A.I.S.S. (Gene-Based Artificial Intelligence Substrate System) initiative began as an investigation into overcoming the catastrophic forgetting and rigid structural limitations of traditional deep learning classifiers. By moving away from static backpropagation weights toward a biomimetic, gene-based weight-kernel plasticity model, the architecture established a dynamic runtime capable of real-time adaptation without parameter drift.

## Key Milestone Timeline
- **Phase I (Concept & Simulation):** Initial algorithmic modeling of gene-expression matrices and localized resonance scoring gates.
- **Phase II (SAIHV Integration):** Development of System Agnostic Hardware Virtualization (SAIHV) to bind runtime execution seamlessly across heterogeneous CPU and GPU targets.
- **Phase III (Empirical Validation & Benchmarking):** Multi-node execution testing on laptop and desktop RTX hardware profiles, culminating in verifiable plasticity deltas (0.0989) and complete state-reset verification.
- **Phase IV (IP Formalization):** Compilation of the 18-part technical documentation suite to establish an unassailable professional IP paper trail.
